{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "weno3_eno1_D1y_extrapolate_bc",
    "description": "Hybrid WENO3/ENO1 upwind first derivative d u/dy on cartesian_uniform_2d with the linear-extrapolation halo closure -- the per-axis derivative diff2y of NCAR fire_behavior's level-set scheme fire_upwinding = 8 (physics/level_set_mod.F90 Calc_tend_ls case 8; Munoz-Esparza et al. 2018): third-order Jiang-Shu WENO3 in a band of weno_band_cells cells around the zero level set, first-order ENO1 (minmod) elsewhere and within bdy_eno1_cells of the walls, upwind side of the WENO reconstruction chosen by the wind-relative propagation sign signo = (grad4 u . (wind_u, wind_v)) d4 u/dy. It is rules/weno5_eno1_D1y_extrapolate_bc with Select_weno5 replaced by Select_weno3 -- case (8)'s in-band branch is case (9)'s with that one substitution, and the band test, the wall test and the selector are byte-for-byte the same. This is the quantity fire_behavior feeds both to |grad psi| = sqrt(diff2x^2 + diff2y^2) and to the front normal nvy = diff2y/sqrt(|grad psi|^2 + eps); rules/weno3_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc packages the norm as a compound match so a consumer may write the norm as sqrt((D(u,x))^2 + (D(u,y))^2) and the normal's ingredients as bare D(u, wrt: x) / D(u, wrt: y) with the same scheme. Match D(u, wrt: y) at priority 10 (before the priority-less centered central_D1y_* rules if a document imports both), scoped with where {{u: {{shape: [x, y]}}}} (esm-spec 9.6.1). A document must import this rule OR its WENO5 sibling, never both: they match the same pattern at the same priority. THE SCHEME: the makearray tiles [x, y] into 7 regions, the same seven the WENO5 sibling uses, with the doubly-interior block one cell wider on each side of y because the WENO3 stencil reaches two cells rather than three -- [3, NX-2] x [3, NY-2] is a single apply of the imported stencils/weno3_eno1_D1y_interior (the hybrid switch on the field and the index, see that stencil); the strips where the fourth-order selector or the five-point WENO stencil would leave the domain are plain ENO1 minmod; and the two outermost lines along y are ENO1 against a one-cell guarded linear-extrapolation ghost, stencils/extrapolate_linear_ghost, fire_behavior's Extrapol_var_at_bdys (HALO = 1, BIAS = 1). FREE NAMES: wind_u, wind_v ([x, y] fields), bdy_eno1_cells (>= 2), weno_band_cells, and the grid's dx, dy. Requires NX, NY >= 5. ORDER: formally first; third in the smooth WENO band. EXACT on a linear field.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:rule",
      "family:finite_difference",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_2d",
      "op:D",
      "axes:x,y",
      "order:1",
      "stencil_width:5",
      "bc:extrapolate_linear",
      "spec:9.6.8"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Munoz-Esparza, D., Kosovic, B., Jimenez, P. A., & Coen, J. L. (2018). An accurate fire-spread algorithm in the Weather Research and Forecasting model using the level-set method. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 10(4), 908-926. The hybrid WENO/ENO1 level-set schemes (fire_upwinding = 8 and 9) of WRF-Fire / NCAR fire_behavior.",
        "doi": "10.1002/2017MS001108"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Jiang, G.-S., & Shu, C.-W. (1996). Efficient implementation of weighted ENO schemes. Journal of Computational Physics, 126(1), 202-228. The classical WENO smoothness indicators beta_k and nonlinear weights w_k = gamma_k/(beta_k + eps)^2.",
        "doi": "10.1006/jcph.1996.0130"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Harten, A., Engquist, B., Osher, S., & Chakravarthy, S. R. (1987). Uniformly high order accurate essentially non-oscillatory schemes, III. Journal of Computational Physics, 71(2), 231-303. ENO stencil selection by smallest divided difference (first-order form: the minmod limiter).",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(87)90031-3"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR Community Fire Behavior Model (fire_behavior), develop @ eb77580, physics/level_set_mod.F90: Calc_tend_ls, Select_eno, Select_weno3, Select_4th, Select_godunov (case 4), Extrapol_var_at_bdys. The transcribed reference implementation."
      }
    ]
  },
